"""Complete the existing ten sections; retain navigation, links and approved-user architecture."""
from pathlib import Path
import re,html,json
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
# Each entry adds independently authored teaching and explicit assessment-family coverage.
DATA=[
('Identify a solid using its faces, edges and vertices.','Think of a cereal carton and a die. Both have flat faces, but only the die has six equal square faces.', 'Build a triangular prism with two card triangles and three rectangular strips. Mark each corner with a sticky dot; feel each join without counting it twice.', [('A cube has6 square faces. Count4 edges around the top,4 around the bottom and4 joining them:12 edges. Four top corners and4 bottom corners give8 vertices.','6 faces,12 edges,8 vertices'),('A triangular prism has2 triangular ends and3 rectangular sides:5 faces. Each end has3 corners, giving6 vertices. Three edges on each end plus3 joining edges gives9 edges.','5 faces,9 edges,6 vertices')], 'How many edges does a triangular prism have?',9,'Three on each triangular end and three joining the ends:3+3+3=9.', 'Compare a pentagonal prism with a triangular prism. Explain how the number of side rectangles follows the number of sides on each end.', 'Use coloured stickers for vertices and a pencil mark on each counted edge. Turn the model to include hidden parts.', 'For a pentagonal prism, justify7 faces,15 edges and10 vertices without relying on a memorised list.'),
('Predict and explain how a net folds to make an object.','A flattened carton still contains the faces that made the box. Which joins did you cut, and which became folds?', 'Cut six equal paper squares. Tape them edge to edge in the labelled cross shown. Predict the top, bottom and opposite faces before folding.', [('In the labelled cross, A is opposite E, B is opposite D, and C is opposite F. Use C as a base, lift its neighbours, and track the remaining end face.','Opposite pairs:A–E, B–D, C–F'),('A2-by3 block has6 squares but will not make a cube. Folding forces repeated face directions, so some faces overlap. Counting squares is necessary but not enough.','6 squares does not guarantee a net')], 'How many square faces must a cube net contain?',6,'One square for each of the six faces. Connectivity and fold arrangement must also be checked.', 'Choose another arrangement in the net explorer. Predict its result, test, then explain a changed connection.', 'Label faces before folding and keep one face flat on the table.', 'Find two cube nets that cannot be matched merely by rotating the paper. Explain how you know they are different.'),
('Design, test and improve a net with matching face dimensions.','A packaging designer cannot use six arbitrary rectangles: every closing edge must match its partner.', 'Use squared paper to draw a2-by3-by4 carton. Cut and fold with an adult. Tabs can attach faces, but tabs are not extra faces.', [('Opposite faces come in pairs: two2-by3 rectangles, two2-by4 rectangles, and two3-by4 rectangles. This accounts for all6 faces.','Three pairs of matching rectangles'),('A square-based pyramid uses1 square base and4 triangular faces. The triangle bases match the four square edges; the triangle tips meet at one apex.','1 square+4 triangles=5 faces')], 'How many triangular side faces does a square-based pyramid net need?',4,'The square has four edges, and each edge joins one triangular side face.', 'Explain why a cube net with one square stretched into a rectangle will fail to close as a cube.', 'Write matching edge lengths in the same colour, then compare every joined edge.', 'Design two nets for the same carton. Explain which would waste less surrounding paper, without changing the box dimensions.'),
('Construct and read a grid using horizontal-first coordinates.','A grid-square name locates a region. A coordinate pair locates an exact intersection. Which is more precise for a robot?', 'Lay string in equally spaced horizontal and vertical lines. Mark the origin where the zero lines meet. Use a counter to travel across first and up second.', [('From(0,0), move4 right and2 up. The intersection is(4,2). A point at(2,4) is a different position because the movements are reversed.','(4,2):4 across,2 up'),('Points(0,3) and(3,0) lie on different axes. A zero does not mean the point is missing: it means no movement along that direction.','Zero is a valid coordinate')], 'A point is3 across and5 up. What is its FIRST coordinate?',3,'The first coordinate is horizontal position, so the ordered pair is(3,5).', 'Plot(1,5),(5,1) and(3,3). Explain which has equal horizontal and vertical coordinates.', 'Say “across, then up” and trace one move at a time. Keep grid intervals equal.', 'Construct your own first-quadrant grid and specify its unit spacing. Ask a partner to plot five points from your instructions.'),
('Follow, construct and check a route using coordinates and directions.','Giving a final destination is different from giving a route. The same place may be reached by different sequences of moves.', 'Use a toy courier on a floor grid. Move it right, up and left, recording the position after every instruction.', [('Start at(1,2). Right4 reaches(5,2); up3 reaches(5,5); left2 reaches(3,5). The net movement is2 right and3 up.','Final position:(3,5)'),('Start at(4,6). Down2 reaches(4,4), then left3 reaches(1,4). Check that the whole route remains within the map.','Final position:(1,4)')], 'Start at(2,3) and move4 right. What is the final FIRST coordinate?',6,'Horizontal position changes from2 to2+4=6. The final point is(6,3).', 'Find two different routes from(2,2) to(6,5). Describe which is shorter in grid units and justify any tie.', 'Record a small table with columns “move”, “across” and “up”.', 'Plan a route through three landmarks while avoiding a blocked grid segment. Explain how you checked every step.'),
('Translate a shape while preserving every side length and angle.','A sticker slides across a page without spinning. Can one corner move a different distance and still count as the same slide?', 'Trace a card triangle on transparent paper. Slide the paper without turning it. Join each original vertex to its image and compare the three movement arrows.', [('Move(1,1),(3,1),(2,3) by2 right and1 up. The images are(3,2),(5,2),(4,4). Every vertex receives the same movement.','Add2 across and1 up to EVERY vertex'),('A marker moves from(2,3) to(5,5). The translation is3 right and2 up. Applying this movement to(4,1) gives(7,3).','Movement:3 right,2 up')], 'A vertex at(2,4) moves3 right and1 up. What is its new FIRST coordinate?',5,'Add3 to the horizontal coordinate and1 to the vertical coordinate, giving(5,5).', 'Describe a translation that takes one drawn triangle onto its partner. Verify it with all three vertices.', 'Use equal-length arrows pointing in the same direction from corresponding vertices.', 'Show a supposed translation where one vertex moved differently. Explain why the result is distorted rather than translated.'),
('Reflect points and shapes using equal perpendicular distances.','Think of a reflection in a still pond. A point and its image are equally far from the mirror, on opposite sides.', 'Fold paper along a straight line. Mark a point and press it through the fold to find its partner. Unfold and compare distances measured straight across the mirror.', [('Reflect(3,2) in x=5. It is2 units left of the mirror, so the image is2 units right at(7,2). The mirror lies halfway between the pair.','(3,2) →(7,2) across x=5'),('Reflect(4,1) in y=3. The point is2 units below the line, so the image is2 above it at(4,5). The horizontal coordinate stays4.','(4,1) →(4,5) across y=3')], 'Point(2,1) reflects across x=4. What is its image FIRST coordinate?',6,'The point is2 units left of x=4, so its image is at x=6. The image is(6,1).', 'Draw a triangle touching the mirror line. Explain why vertices on the line do not move.', 'Draw a short perpendicular connector from each vertex to the mirror before locating its partner.', 'Create a reflected logo that looks different from a translated copy. Explain which feature demonstrates the reversal.'),
('Rotate around a stated centre and investigate symmetry.','A windmill spins around its hub. Moving the hub changes where every blade travels, even if the turn angle stays the same.', 'Trace a square on transparent paper. Pin the centre safely with adult help, then make quarter-turns. Count each position that matches the original outline.', [('A square matches after90°,180°,270° and360°, so its order is4. The smallest positive matching turn is90°. Count the full turn once, not zero and360° as two separate positions.','Square:order4'),('A non-square rectangle first matches after180° and again at360°, giving order2. An equilateral triangle has3 matches; a shape with no smaller match has order1.','Rectangle:order2')], 'What is the rotational symmetry order of a non-square rectangle?',2,'It matches twice in one full revolution:180° and360°.', 'Use the symmetry explorer to compare a square, rectangle and scalene triangle. Test a possible mirror line as well as a rotation.', 'Mark the centre and keep a dashed original outline for comparison.', 'Explain why a line passing through the centre is not automatically a line of symmetry. Find a counterexample.'),
('Create and test yes/no decisions that classify shapes by properties.','A computer cannot reliably answer “does it look like a square?” Which observable properties would make a reliable decision?', 'Sort card shapes using yes/no questions. Put squares, non-square rectangles and non-square rhombuses in your collection so your rule faces genuine counterexamples.', [('A square has4 equal sides AND4 right angles. Equal sides alone can also describe a non-square rhombus. Right angles alone can describe a non-square rectangle.','Both conditions are needed'),('A solid with6 faces is not necessarily a cube. A non-cube rectangular prism has6 faces too. Check whether all six faces are squares, with matching equal edges.','Test a rule using counterexamples')], 'How many right angles must a square have?',4,'All four corners are right angles, and all four side lengths are equal.', 'Build a two-decision rule in the classifier. Explain which shapes still share a group and what further question would separate them.', 'Use a property table: dimension, side count, right angles, equal sides and curved surfaces.', 'Repair a faulty rule without excluding valid boundary cases. Explain why every square is also a rectangle.'),
('Combine a net, route and transformation in a justified design.','A park planner needs a kiosk model, a delivery route and a mirrored logo. How can separate checks protect one complete plan?', 'Use grid paper and card. Build a kiosk from a valid prism net, place its map point at an intersection, then trace and transform a sign nearby.', [('A courier starts at(2,2), moves4 right and3 up to(6,5), then2 left to(4,5). Check each intermediate position before accepting the destination.','Check the route step by step'),('A sign point at(2,3) moves2 right to(4,3), then reflects in x=5 to(6,3). The slide must happen first; reversing the steps gives a different result.','Order of transformations matters')], 'A point at(2,3) moves2 right, then reflects across x=5. What is its final FIRST coordinate?',6,'First reach(4,3). This lies1 unit left of x=5, so reflect1 unit right to(6,3).', 'Design a10-by10 park with3 landmarks, a checked kiosk net and two transformed motifs. Label inputs, results and a reason for every decision.', 'Make a checklist for face connections, coordinate order, movement and classification properties.', 'Swap designs with a partner and find a counterexample to one unchecked claim. Revise the design using evidence.')]

def normal(s):return re.sub(r'(?<=[A-Za-z])(?=\d)|(?<=\d)(?=[A-Za-z])',' ',s)
def main():
 p=ROOT/'space-lessons.html';s=p.read_text()
 if 'data-space-complete="1"' in s:print('Completed lesson content preserved.');return
 sections=re.findall(r'<section id="lesson-(\d+)".*?</section>',s,re.S);assert len(sections)==10
 for i,data in enumerate(DATA,1):
  goal,prior,concrete,examples,question,answer,solution,independent,support,extension=map(lambda x:normal(x) if isinstance(x,str) else x,data)
  match=re.search(r'<section id="lesson-'+str(i)+r'".*?</section>',s,re.S);old=match.group(0);head=old[:old.index('<h3>Explore and learn')];head=re.sub(r'(<h3>Learning goal</h3>)<p>.*?</p>',lambda m:m[1]+'<p>'+html.escape(goal)+'</p>',head)
  intro=re.search(r'<h3>Explore and learn</h3><p>(.*?)</p>',old,re.S).group(1);mis=re.search(r'<h3>Common misconception</h3><p>(.*?)</p>',old,re.S).group(1)
  if i==7:mis='A reflected shape is not merely slid across the mirror. Match each point by equal perpendicular distances; a symmetrical shape may still look unchanged.'
  extra='<h3>Connect what you know</h3><p>'+html.escape(prior)+'</p><h3>Explore with objects</h3><p>'+html.escape(concrete)+'</p><h3>Connect the model to mathematical language</h3><p>'+intro+'</p>'
  for j,(ex,conclusion) in enumerate(examples,1):extra+='<div class="worked"><h3>Worked example '+str(j)+'</h3><p>'+html.escape(normal(ex))+'</p><p><strong>'+html.escape(normal(conclusion))+'</strong></p></div>'
  tools={1:'space-classification.html',2:'space-net-explorer.html',3:'space-net-explorer.html',4:'space-coordinates.html',5:'space-coordinates.html',6:'space-lab.html',7:'space-lab.html',8:'space-symmetry.html',9:'space-classification.html',10:'space-lab.html'}
  extra+='<p><a class="tool-link" href="'+tools[i]+'">Open the interactive model for this lesson</a></p>'
  if i in [2,3]:extra+='<figure id="net-model-'+str(i)+'"></figure>'
  if i in [4,5]:extra+='<figure id="point-model-'+str(i)+'"></figure>'
  extra+='<h3>Predict and check</h3><div class="practice"><label>'+html.escape(question)+' <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Lesson '+str(i)+' practice answer"></label><button type="button" data-answer="'+str(answer)+'" data-solution="'+html.escape(solution,quote=True)+'">Check my answer</button><p class="practice-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p></div><h3>Common misconception</h3><p>'+mis+'</p><h3>Try independently and explain why</h3><p>'+html.escape(independent)+'</p><h3>A helpful starting point</h3><p>'+html.escape(support)+'</p><h3>Take it further</h3><p>'+html.escape(extension)+'</p><h3>Exit reflection</h3><p>Explain one decision you checked today. What evidence would convince a partner, and which mistake would you now notice?</p>'
  if i==9:extra+='<p>Shape-classification algorithms are an enrichment activity supporting reasoning; they are not a fourth descriptor in the final Year 5 Space curriculum.</p>'
  new=head+extra+'</section>';s=s[:match.start()]+new+s[match.end():]
 s=s.replace('</style>', '.worked{border-left:5px solid #087f7a;padding:.8rem;background:#eef8f5}.practice input,.practice button{font:inherit;max-width:100%;padding:.6rem}.practice-feedback.correct{color:#166534;background:#dcfce7}.practice-feedback.incorrect{color:#991b1b;background:#fee2e2}figure svg{width:100%;max-width:600px;max-height:340px}.tool-link{display:inline-block;padding:.6rem}.practice{margin:1rem 0}</style>',1)
 s=s.replace('</body>','<script src="js/space-diagrams.js"></script><script src="js/space-lessons.js"></script></body>');p.write_text(s)
main()
